"""Orchestrator — owns the LLM iteration loop for one turn.

Responsibilities
----------------
* Build a fresh ``PromptContext`` on **every** iteration from the live
  ``TrackerHandle`` so that the system prompt always reflects the current
  stack, memory values, and conversation history.
* Advance deterministic phases before each LLM call.
* Handle three response modes from ``SkillExecutor.advance_steps``:
    - ``WAIT``        — emit text, end turn immediately.
    - ``CONTINUE``    — emit text to tracker history, keep looping.
    - ``rephrase``    — do not emit verbatim; hand off to
                        ``_run_rephrase_call`` which asks the LLM to reword
                        the text naturally and ends the turn.
* Call the LLM, handle the possible response shapes:
    text only              — emit, return.
    text + tool calls      — emit text, dispatch tools, continue.
    tool calls only        — dispatch tools, continue.
    listen only            — do not emit; dispatch listen; return (wait).
    listen mixed with text
    or other tools         — ignore listen; follow text / tools as above.
    neither                — retry a bounded number of times, then return error.
* Dispatch each tool call via SkillExecutor; propagate any agent response
  payloads the tool produces.

The Orchestrator contains no prompt text of its own.  Main-turn,
rephrase, and silent ``set_fields`` fill prompts live in
``PromptConstructor``; skill-exit criteria judging lives in
``CompletionJudge`` (injected into the skill completion gate).

Response emission
-----------------
Responses are not threaded back through a callback. A ``MessageTurnContext``
is established for the duration of the turn and exposed as an ambient
``contextvars.ContextVar``; the orchestrator emits every response with
``await current_turn_context().send(text)``. The same context carries the
per-turn cancellation token by reference, so the loop can check
``is_cancelled`` and stop execution on teardown or explicit cancellation.
In-flight LLM, tool, and skill awaits are raced against that token via
``run_cancellable``. Voice barge-in mutes delivery while execution continues;
the context consumes queued input between iterations.
"""

